I'm Katz eASIC I'm the director of a  short history of the high-rise it's an  interactive documentary that traces the  history of vertical living  when the New York Times approached us at  the National Film Board of Canada to  collaborate potentially collaborate they  told us about this place called the  morgue it's a collection of photographs  that are Undead and you know three  floors below Times Square and I thought  that that would be such a fascinating  way to revisit the history of  essentially how our cities have been  built over time my name is Jeff Roth I  work in what's called the morgue at the  New York Times I kind of manage all the  old stuff it's called the morgue that's  kind of a reporter's parlance for where  all the dead stories Lyerly it was  created back in 1905 by our legendary  managing editor carve Anand and to start  codifying and creating a clippings  library as far as what is down here well  first thing is the clippings collection  dates back to the 1870s there's the  picture library and so that comprises oh  we estimate maybe between 6 and 8  million physical photographs I would  estimate only 1 to 2 percent is  digitized which means 98 to 99 percent  are not digitized there's always a  serendipity of finding something that is  odd or is you know quite historically  valuable oh yeah tell me a little bit  about these um pictures from London and  Russia and all that oh yeah so these are  I mean this is just you know London  housing and then it would be Russia  housing and this I spent about a week in  the morgue it's this incredible  collection of forgotten history the back  of the photographs tell a whole story I  mean it's it tells all the contextual  stuff so it tells you you know when the  photo was published it tells you maybe  the cut line it can tell you what part  of the paper it was published in this is  a great folder I just found another gem  I mean I know you're not surprised but  when I stumbled  this beautiful photograph of the  majestic building sitting you know like  a crown on top of Central Park  I immediately knew that that would be  the opening shot of the entire series  what's cool about our process is that I  really didn't know what the film would  be until I spent time with the material  in the morgue so I didn't come in with a  preconceived notion which is for me a  real documentary process  nothing beats getting lost in a  collection of 6 million photographs and  finding your way out with a really  fascinating story
 

